1. AIJ pedestrian-wind benchmark data
-------------------------------------

Publisher:  Architectural Institute of Japan (AIJ), CFD benchmark working group
Source:     https://www.aij.or.jp/jpn/publish/cfdguide/index_e.htm

The wind-tunnel measurements and geometry definitions published by AIJ for the
guideline benchmark cases are used as validation targets for AeroFlow's Cases A, C
and E (docs/VALIDATION.md, V12-V15).

The DERIVED files listed in 1d below — measurement values and geometry converted
into JSON and glTF for the solver — are redistributed here with AIJ's agreement, on
the terms in 1a. The ORIGINAL AIJ workbooks and DXF archive are NOT: they stay
gitignored inputs to the offline converters in scripts/. Download them from the AIJ
URL above and re-run the conversion scripts to regenerate the derived files; the
SHA-256 values in 1d identify the exact inputs used.

The published geometry parameters of the benchmark configurations (for example Case
A's 1:1:2 prism with b = 0.08 m at 1/250 scale) are stated in the Tominaga et al.
paper cited below and are used here as ordinary factual citations.


1a. Terms AIJ set for these data
--------------------------------

Whether these data may be redistributed at all was put to the AIJ CFD guideline
working group directly. Prof. Hideki Kikumoto (Institute of Industrial Science, The
University of Tokyo) answered for the group in August 2026: no objection in
principle to redistributing files derived from the benchmark data, provided three
things are clearly stated and followed.

  1. Users of the benchmark data cite the data paper AND the original publications
     describing the individual benchmark cases and experiments (see 1b below).
  2. It is clearly stated that derived files were created and processed
     independently, and that AIJ does not guarantee them (see 1c below).
  3. Derived files retain attribution and provenance: source, download date, and
     any processing or conversion performed.

These are treated as binding terms, not as a style preference, and they are enforced
by code rather than by this file. Every derived file in 1d carries a machine-readable
`attribution` block — data paper, case publications, dataset of record, LES
guideline, processing, and AIJ's disclaimer verbatim — the converters in scripts/
write that block into everything they generate, and the loaders in
packages/core/src/validation/ REFUSE TO LOAD an AIJ fixture that has lost or altered
it (aijAttribution.ts). A benchmark file cannot reach a score unattributed, here or
on your machine. The citation and disclaimer also render beside the score in the app
and are copied into every exported run report, so a downloaded artifact is
self-describing. The decision record is
docs/decisions/D2-aij-data-redistribution.md.


1b. How to cite these data
--------------------------

If you use the AIJ benchmark data in this repository — the fixtures, the converted
geometry, or numbers scored against them — or obtain it from AIJ yourself, cite the
data paper AND the original publication(s) for the individual case. The data paper
itself requires this; citing AIJ alone does not satisfy it.

Data paper (cite for any AIJ benchmark case):

  Kikumoto, H., Okaze, T., Yoshie, R., Tachibana, T., Ishihara, T., Nonomura, Y.,
  Kiyota, N., Kondo, H., Mochida, A. & Tominaga, Y. (2026), "Comprehensive
  Experimental Database for Validating CFD Simulations in Urban Wind Environment:
  Benchmark Cases Curated by the Architectural Institute of Japan", Japan
  Architectural Review 9(1), e70083. https://doi.org/10.1002/2475-8876.70083

Guideline papers behind the benchmark suite:

  Tominaga, Y., Mochida, A., Yoshie, R., Kataoka, H., Nozu, T., Yoshikawa, M. &
  Shirasawa, T. (2008), "AIJ guidelines for practical applications of CFD to
  pedestrian wind environment around buildings", J. Wind Eng. Ind. Aerodyn.
  96(10-11), 1749-1761. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jweia.2008.02.058

  Okaze, T., Kikumoto, H., Ikegaya, N., Nakao, K., Ono, H., Nakajima, K., Imano, M.,
  Hasama, T., Tabata, Y., Kishida, T., Yoshie, R. & Tominaga, Y. (2026), "AIJ
  guidelines on the applications of large-eddy simulation to pedestrian wind
  environment: Recommendations and validation benchmarks", J. Wind Eng. Ind.
  Aerodyn. 269, 106321. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jweia.2025.106321
  — the reference for implementing and evaluating these benchmark cases with LES,
  which is what AeroFlow's solver is. AIJ asked specifically that it be consulted
  alongside Tominaga et al. (2008).

Per case:

  Case A (isolated 1:1:2 prism)
    Experiment:  Meng, Y. & Hibi, K. (1998), "Turbulent measurements of the flow
                 field around a high-rise building", Wind Engineers, JAWE 1998(76),
                 55-64. https://doi.org/10.5359/jawe.1998.76_55
    Dataset:     AIJ UWE Benchmark Dataset - Case A (112), Ishihara, Hibi, Tominaga,
                 Kikumoto & Okaze (2025), CC BY 4.0.
                 https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15050148

  Case C (simple city blocks, 2H center building)
    Experiment:  Nonomura, Y., Kobayashi, N., Tominaga, Y. & Mochida, A. (2003),
                 "The cross comparison of CFD prediction for flow field around
                 building blocks (part 3)", Summaries of Technical Papers of Annual
                 Meeting, Japan Association for Wind Engineering 2003, 41.
                 https://doi.org/10.14887/jaweam.2003.0.41.0
    Dataset:     AIJ UWE Benchmark Dataset - Case C (Blocks), Nonomura, Tominaga,
                 Kikumoto & Okaze (2025), CC BY 4.0.
                 https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15401792

  Case E (Niigata, actual urban area)
    Experiment:  Yoshie, R., Mochida, A., Tominaga, Y., Kataoka, H., Harimoto, K.,
                 Nozu, T. & Shirasawa, T. (2007), "Cooperative project for CFD
                 prediction of pedestrian wind environment in the Architectural
                 Institute of Japan", J. Wind Eng. Ind. Aerodyn. 95(9-11),
                 1551-1578. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jweia.2007.02.023
                 and AIJ (2020), Guidebook for CFD Predictions of Urban Wind
                 Environment (in Japanese), ISBN 978-4-8189-2718-6.
    Dataset:     AIJ UWE Benchmark Dataset - Case E (Niigata), Tominaga, Kikumoto &
                 Okaze (2025), CC BY 4.0. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15429018

AIJ has openly republished these measurements per case on Zenodo under CC BY 4.0.
Those records are each case's dataset of record and are cited as such above. The
files here were derived from the workbooks on the AIJ guidebook page at the SHA-256
values recorded in 1d, NOT from the Zenodo copies, so they are cited as the case's
dataset rather than claimed as the input actually used.


